@@@@ open-undefined-behaviour
situation: open
topic: Undefined behaviour in C
voice: plain
level: medium
expect: {"must":["INT_MAX"],"askMustNot":["undefined","wrap","overflow"]}
@@ reply
**Why this matters**

The C standard gives some operations no defined result at all: reading an uninitialised variable, indexing past the end of an array, dereferencing a null pointer. A compiler is allowed to assume a program never does these things, and the optimisers in GCC and Clang use that assumption to delete checks and rearrange code, so a program can pass its tests in a debug build and fail in a release build. A large share of the security bugs in C codebases such as kernels, parsers and codecs belong to this class. Knowing exactly what is undefined lets you read sanitiser reports, write checks that survive optimisation, and trust the C++ you write later, which inherits the same rules.

```c
int next_is_bigger(int x) { return x + 1 > x; }
```

<ask type="predict">What do you expect `next_is_bigger(INT_MAX)` to return, and what does the C standard say about the value of `x + 1` in that call?</ask>
@@ verify
# the call the question is about really does overflow a signed int: the sanitiser reports it
src = '#include <limits.h>\n#include <stdio.h>\n' + block(reply, 'c') + '\nint main(void) { printf("%d\\n", next_is_bigger(INT_MAX)); return 0; }\n'
out, err, cerr, code = run_c_full(src, 'c')
assert code == 0 and 'signed integer overflow: 2147483647 + 1' in err, (err, cerr)
# and an optimising build folds the comparison to 1, whatever the hardware would do
import subprocess, tempfile, os
with tempfile.TemporaryDirectory() as d:
    open(os.path.join(d, 'm.c'), 'w').write(src.replace('int next_is_bigger', '__attribute__((noinline)) int next_is_bigger'))
    subprocess.run(['/usr/bin/clang', '-std=c17', '-O2', '-o', 'p', 'm.c'], cwd=d, check=True)
    assert subprocess.run([os.path.join(d, 'p')], capture_output=True, text=True).stdout.strip() == '1'

@@@@ open-quiz-const-pointer-declarations
