@@@@ correct-recall-static-then-linkage-opening
situation: after-correct
topic: Linkage in C: static and extern
voice: wordy
level: medium
before: {"recall":[{"prompt":"What does `static` do to a function defined at file scope in C?","topic":"Linkage in C: static and extern","reviews":1,"lapses":0}]}
expect: {"verdict":"correct","heading":"Why this matters","askTypes":["predict","short","explain","choice"],"must":["internal linkage"],"askMustNot":["duplicate","twice","two definitions","multiple definition"],"forbid":["solution"]}
@@ tutor
<ask type="short">What does `static` do to a function defined at file scope in C?</ask>
@@ learner answer sure
So I remember this one from the helper functions I wrote last week. Putting static on a function at file scope gives the function internal linkage, so the name is only visible inside that .c file and the linker never gets to see it. The practical upshot is that another file can define its own function with the same name, and the two don't clash at link time.
@@ reply
<grade verdict="correct" score="1">"internal linkage, so the name is only visible inside that .c file" is the rule, and the consequence you gave, that two files can each have a function of that name, is what it is used for.</grade>

**Why this matters**

Linkage decides which names the linker can see, and so which declarations in different `.c` files refer to the same function or variable. It is how a C library keeps its helper functions private, how one global is shared between files through a header and `extern`, and where "undefined symbol" errors at link time come from. The same rules sit under namespaces, `inline` and the one-definition rule in C++.

<ask type="predict">A header `config.h` contains the line `int verbose = 0;`. Both `main.c` and `log.c` include it; they are compiled separately and then linked into one program. What does the linker report, and why?</ask>
@@ verify
import subprocess, tempfile, os
with tempfile.TemporaryDirectory() as d:
    w = lambda n, s: open(os.path.join(d, n), 'w').write(s)
    w('config.h', 'int verbose = 0;\n')
    w('main.c', '#include "config.h"\nint log_level(void);\nint main(void) { return verbose + log_level(); }\n')
    w('log.c', '#include "config.h"\nint log_level(void) { return verbose; }\n')
    p = subprocess.run(['/usr/bin/clang', '-std=c17', 'main.c', 'log.c', '-o', 'p'], cwd=d, capture_output=True, text=True)
    assert p.returncode != 0 and 'duplicate symbol' in p.stderr and 'verbose' in p.stderr, p.stderr
    # the learner's claim: two files may each define a static function of the same name
    w('a.c', 'static int helper(void) { return 1; }\nint from_a(void) { return helper(); }\n')
    w('b.c', 'static int helper(void) { return 2; }\nint from_a(void);\nint main(void) { return helper() + from_a() - 3; }\n')
    p = subprocess.run(['/usr/bin/clang', '-std=c17', 'a.c', 'b.c', '-o', 'q'], cwd=d, capture_output=True, text=True)
    assert p.returncode == 0, p.stderr
    assert subprocess.run([os.path.join(d, 'q')]).returncode == 0

@@@@ correct-probe-reference-then-plan
situation: after-correct
topic: References versus pointers in C++
voice: terse
level: medium
expect: {"verdict":"correct","setsProblem":true,"askTypes":["code"],"need":["plan","note"],"must":["\\b17\\b"],"mustNot":["cannot be null"]}
@@ tutor
```cpp
int a = 1, b = 2;
int &r = a;
r = b;
b = 9;
```

<ask type="short">After these four lines, what are the values of `a` and `b`, and what does `r` refer to?</ask>
@@ learner answer sure
a = 2, b = 9. r still refers to a. r = b copies b's value into a, you can't rebind a reference.
@@ reply
<grade verdict="correct" score="1">"r still refers to a" and "r = b copies b's value into a" are both right: a reference is bound once, when it is initialised, and from then on every use of `r` is a use of `a`.</grade>

<plan>
- References as function parameters
- What a pointer can do that a reference cannot
- Dangling references
</plan>

A reference parameter is another name for the caller's own variable, so the function can change it with no `&` at the call and no `*` inside:

```cpp
#include <iostream>

void add_ten(int &n) { n += 10; }

int main() {
    int total = 7;
    add_ten(total);
    std::cout << total << "\n";
    return 0;
}
```

```
17
```

<note>A reference must be initialised where it is declared and names that one object for its whole life: `r = b` assigns to the object, it never re-binds. A parameter `T &x` lets a function modify the caller's argument; `const T &x` lets it read the argument without copying it.</note>

**Problem 1 · warm-up**

<solution>void swap_ints(int &x, int &y) { int t = x; x = y; y = t; } With a = 3 and b = 8 the program prints 8 3.</solution>

<ask type="code" lang="cpp">Write `void swap_ints(int &x, int &y)` that exchanges the caller's two variables. In `main`, set `a = 3` and `b = 8`, call it once, and print `a` and `b` with a space between them.</ask>
@@ verify
# the probe: values after the four lines
out = run_cpp('#include <iostream>\nint main() {\n' + block(tutor[0], 'cpp') + 'std::cout << a << " " << b << " " << (&r == &a) << "\\n"; return 0; }\n')
assert out.split() == ['2', '9', '1'], out
# the reference solution for the task set
ref = '#include <iostream>\nvoid swap_ints(int &x, int &y) { int t = x; x = y; y = t; }\nint main() { int a = 3, b = 8; swap_ints(a, b); std::cout << a << " " << b << "\\n"; return 0; }\n'
assert run_cpp(ref).strip() == '8 3'
assert '8 3' in solution(reply) and '8 3' not in visible(reply)

@@@@ correct-sizeof-unevaluated-operand
situation: after-correct
topic: sizeof and the sizes of types in C
options: {"difficulty":"hard"}
voice: terse
level: hard
expect: {"verdict":"correct","setsProblem":true,"askTypes":["problem"],"must":["Problem 2"],"askMustNot":["not evaluated","unevaluated","\\b8\\b"]}
@@ tutor
**Problem 1 · hard**

On a platform where `int` and `float` are 4 bytes and `double` is 8 bytes, give the value of each of these in C (not C++): `sizeof(char)`, `sizeof('a')`, `sizeof(1.0f)` and `sizeof(1.0)`.

<solution>sizeof(char) is 1 by definition. In C a character constant has type int, so sizeof('a') is 4. 1.0f is a float: 4. 1.0 with no suffix is a double: 8.</solution>
<check lang="python">
import ctypes
assert [ctypes.sizeof(ctypes.c_char), ctypes.sizeof(ctypes.c_int), ctypes.sizeof(ctypes.c_float), ctypes.sizeof(ctypes.c_double)] == [1, 4, 4, 8]
</check>

<ask type="problem">The four values, with the type of each operand.</ask>
@@ learner answer sure
1, 4, 4, 8. char is 1 by definition. 'a' is an int in C so 4. 1.0f is a float, 4. 1.0 has no suffix so it's a double, 8.
@@ reply
<grade verdict="correct" score="1">All four are right, and "'a' is an int in C so 4" is the one that is usually missed: a character constant has type `int` in C, though in C++ it is a `char` of size 1.</grade>

**Problem 2 · harder**

```c
int i = 5;
size_t a = sizeof(i++);
size_t b = sizeof(i + 2.0);
```

Same platform. Give the values of `a` and `b`, and the value of `i` after these three lines.

<solution>sizeof uses only the type of its operand and does not evaluate it (a variable-length array is the one exception). i++ has type int, so a = 4, and the increment never happens: i is still 5. In i + 2.0 the int is converted to double, so the expression has type double and b = 8.</solution>
<check lang="python">
import ctypes
assert ctypes.sizeof(ctypes.c_int) == 4 and ctypes.sizeof(ctypes.c_double) == 8
</check>

<ask type="problem">The values of a and b and the final value of i, with the reason for each.</ask>
@@ verify
src = '#include <stdio.h>\nint main(void) {\n' + block(reply, 'c') + 'printf("%zu %zu %d %zu %zu %zu %zu\\n", a, b, i, sizeof(char), sizeof(\'a\'), sizeof(1.0f), sizeof(1.0));\nreturn 0; }\n'
out, err, cerr, code = run_c_full(src, 'c')
assert code == 0 and out.split() == ['4', '8', '5', '1', '4', '4', '8'], (out, cerr)
# clang itself says the increment is not evaluated
assert 'unevaluated' in cerr
# in C++ the character constant is a char
assert run_cpp('#include <cstdio>\nint main() { std::printf("%zu\\n", sizeof(\'a\')); return 0; }\n').strip() == '1'
s = solution(reply)
assert 'a = 4' in s and 'b = 8' in s and 'still 5' in s

@@@@ correct-unsure-switch-fall-through
situation: after-correct
topic: switch and fall-through in C
voice: typos
level: easy
expect: {"verdict":"correct","setsProblem":true,"askTypes":["problem","predict"],"must":["Problem 2"],"askMustNot":["\\bbc\\b"]}
@@ tutor
**Problem 1 · warm-up**

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void) {
    int n = 2;
    switch (n) {
        case 1: printf("one ");
        case 2: printf("two ");
        case 3: printf("three ");
            break;
        default: printf("other ");
    }
    printf("\n");
    return 0;
}
```

<solution>Execution jumps to case 2, prints "two ", falls through into case 3 because there is no break, prints "three ", then the break leaves the switch. Output: two three</solution>

<ask type="problem">What does this program print? Say which statements run and why.</ask>
@@ learner answer unsure
two three i think? it jumps to case 2 and prints two, then theres no break so it carrys on into case 3 and prints three, then the break stops it before default. not totaly sure about the carrying on bit
@@ reply
<grade verdict="correct" score="1">"theres no break so it carrys on into case 3" is exactly what happens: a case label is only a place to jump to, and execution runs on through the labels below it until a `break` or the end of the switch.</grade>

One more at the same level, since you were not sure of the carrying on.

**Problem 2 · same level**

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void) {
    int n = 3;
    switch (n) {
        case 1: printf("a");
        default: printf("b");
        case 4: printf("c");
            break;
        case 5: printf("d");
    }
    printf("\n");
    return 0;
}
```

<solution>No case matches 3, so execution jumps to default, wherever it is written. It prints "b", falls through into case 4, prints "c", and the break leaves the switch. Output: bc</solution>

<ask type="problem">What does this one print? Say where execution enters the switch and where it leaves.</ask>
@@ verify
assert run_c(block(tutor[0], 'c')).strip() == 'two three'
assert run_c(block(reply, 'c')).strip() == 'bc'
assert 'Output: bc' in solution(reply) and 'two three' in solution(tutor[0])
